# Who must review a change. Team handles are placeholders: replace
# @your-org with your GitHub organization (dbt/models/groups.yml holds the
# same handles, and the governance lint checks that the two agree).
#
# How GitHub reads this file: the last matching line wins, and when a line
# lists several teams, an approval from ANY ONE of them is enough. So a file
# that holds a team's rules is owned by that team alone. Where all listed
# teams must approve, use a repository ruleset requiring each team instead.
#
# Turn on "Require review from Code Owners" in branch protection, or none of
# this is enforced.

# Default: the data platform team keeps the repo running.
*                                                  @your-org/data-platform

# Business rules. The lint requires each file's owners to be exactly the
# owners of the metrics whose rules it holds (hidden building blocks count
# for the certified metrics that use them).
/dbt/models/semantic/metrics_finance.yml           @your-org/finance
/dbt/models/semantic/metrics_supply_chain.yml      @your-org/supply-chain-ops
/dbt/models/semantic/fct_inventory_batch.yml       @your-org/supply-chain-ops
/dbt/models/semantic/fct_transfers.yml             @your-org/supply-chain-ops
/dbt/models/semantic/fct_goods_received.yml        @your-org/supply-chain-ops
/dbt/models/semantic/fct_returns.yml               @your-org/finance
# fct_sales.yml holds revenue rules (finance) and units sold (supply chain).
/dbt/models/semantic/fct_sales.yml                 @your-org/finance @your-org/supply-chain-ops
